{
  "summary": "Skip calling ForgetTxHash on rejected transactions when the rejection reason is TX_RECONSIDERABLE and the transaction has no witness data. This prevents accidental deletion of pending txid-based orphan resolution requests that share the same hash as the wtxid in non-segwit transactions.",
  "problem": "When a low-feerate parent without a witness is rejected as TX_RECONSIDERABLE, its wtxid is identical to its txid, causing MempoolRejectedTx to wipe queued txid requests intended to resolve orphan children. If that parent transaction arrived from a peer that did not announce the orphan child, 1P1C opportunistic package relay cannot pair them, the child's announcers are never queried for the parent, and the child remains orphaned.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author reports: 'On master the legacy case times out waiting for that request; with the change it passes.' (PR description)"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Small P2P txdownload logic tweak with a functional test addition in p2p_opportunistic_1p1c.py.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Preserves orphan resolution requests for non-witness transactions during opportunistic 1P1C package relay. When a low-feerate parent without a witness is rejected as TX_RECONSIDERABLE, its wtxid equals its txid, inadvertently clearing txid requests queued for peers announcing an orphan child. If the parent arrived from a different peer, package validation fails and the child remains stuck in the orphanage. The PR skips ForgetTxHash for non-witness reconsiderable transactions so child announcers are still queried for the parent by txid, resolving a test timeout bug and matching segwit behavior.",
